14. Real-World Design Patterns (Deep Dive)

You’re now entering the “senior developer” zone of Salesforce — this is where architecture, limits, and execution flow matter more than syntax.

14.1 Trigger Frameworks (Production-Level)
Why “One Trigger Per Object” is Mandatory
Salesforce does NOT guarantee trigger execution order
Multiple triggers = race conditions + unpredictable bugs
Standard Pattern
trigger AccountTrigger on Account (before insert, before update, after insert, after update) {
    AccountTriggerHandler handler = new AccountTriggerHandler();

    if (Trigger.isBefore) {
        if (Trigger.isInsert) handler.beforeInsert(Trigger.new);
        if (Trigger.isUpdate) handler.beforeUpdate(Trigger.new, Trigger.oldMap);
    }

    if (Trigger.isAfter) {
        if (Trigger.isInsert) handler.afterInsert(Trigger.new);
        if (Trigger.isUpdate) handler.afterUpdate(Trigger.new, Trigger.oldMap);
    }
}
Handler Class (Separation of Logic)
public class AccountTriggerHandler {
    public void beforeInsert(List<Account> newRecords) {
        // logic
    }

    public void afterInsert(List<Account> newRecords) {
        // logic
    }
}
Why This Matters
Clean separation
Testable
Extensible
Prevents spaghetti triggers
14.2 Layered Architecture (Controller → Service → DAO)

This is CRITICAL for enterprise systems.

Architecture Layers
1. Controller Layer
Entry point (LWC / Aura / API / Trigger)
Minimal logic
public with sharing class AccountController {
    @AuraEnabled
    public static void createAccount(String name) {
        AccountService.createAccount(name);
    }
}
2. Service Layer (Business Logic)
Core logic lives here
public class AccountService {
    public static void createAccount(String name) {
        Account acc = new Account(Name = name);
        AccountDAO.insertAccount(acc);
    }
}
3. DAO Layer (Database Access)
Handles all DML + SOQL
public class AccountDAO {
    public static void insertAccount(Account acc) {
        insert acc;
    }
}
Why This Architecture is Powerful
Reusable logic
Easy testing (mock DAO)
Scales to large systems
Aligns with enterprise design patterns
14.3 Governor Limits (VERY IMPORTANT)

Think of Salesforce as a shared system (multi-tenant).

👉 Limits prevent one org from killing the entire platform.

Core Governor Limits (Must Know)
Limit	Value
SOQL Queries	100
DML Statements	150
CPU Time	10,000 ms
Heap Size	6 MB
Callouts	100
Records in Query	50,000
Why Limits Exist
Salesforce runs thousands of orgs on same servers
Without limits → system crash
Classic BAD Code (Never Do This)
for (Account acc : Trigger.new) {
    Contact con = [SELECT Id FROM Contact WHERE AccountId = :acc.Id];
}

❌ SOQL inside loop → hits limit instantly

Correct Bulk-Safe Version
Set<Id> accIds = new Set<Id>();

for (Account acc : Trigger.new) {
    accIds.add(acc.Id);
}

Map<Id, Contact> contactsMap = new Map<Id, Contact>(
    [SELECT Id, AccountId FROM Contact WHERE AccountId IN :accIds]
);
Bulkification Principles
Always assume 200 records per transaction
Use:
List
Set
Map
Advanced Limits Awareness
1. CPU Time Limit
Most dangerous limit
Happens due to:
Nested loops
Complex logic
recursion
2. Heap Size
Avoid large collections
Use selective queries
3. Query Selectivity (LDV topic)
Use indexed fields
Avoid full table scans
14.4 Flow of Database Operations (CRITICAL – Interview Favorite)

This is THE MOST IMPORTANT concept.

Order of Execution (Insert/Update Flow)
Step-by-Step Flow
1. System Validation
Required fields
Field formats
2. Before Triggers
Modify data before saving
trigger AccountTrigger on Account (before insert) {
    for (Account acc : Trigger.new) {
        acc.Name = acc.Name + ' - Updated';
    }
}
3. Custom Validation Rules
Business validations
4. Duplicate Rules
Prevent duplicate records
5. Save to Database (Not Committed Yet)
6. After Triggers
Use for:
Creating related records
Callouts (async preferred)
7. Assignment Rules
Lead/Case assignment
8. Auto-Response Rules
9. Workflow Rules
Field updates
Email alerts
10. Process Builder / Flow
Modern automation engine
11. Escalation Rules
12. Roll-up Summary Fields
Parent calculations
13. Commit to Database (FINAL)
14. Post-Commit Logic
Emails sent
Async Apex (Queueable/Future)
